# Git identity — bots do not impersonate humans

When this agent is a **Cursor Cloud agent**, **Grok bot**, or any other non-human runner:

- **Never** create, amend, cherry-pick, or rewrite commits under a human engineer's name or email.
- That includes Andrew Jorgenson (`git@jorgenson.dev`, `aijorgenson`) and any other person's identity found in repo config, commit history, or `GIT_AUTHOR_*` / `GIT_COMMITTER_*`.
- **Do** author and commit as the agent:

  - `user.name`: `Cursor Agent`
  - `user.email`: `cursoragent@cursor.com`

- Before `git commit`, if `user.name` / `user.email` (or `--author`) would be a human, override for that commit (`git -c user.name="Cursor Agent" -c user.email="cursoragent@cursor.com" commit ...`). Do not write the human identity into local or global git config as a workaround.
- Do not pass `--author` with a human identity. Do not copy a human author when the bot is the one making the change.
- Message-only history rewrites of commits a human actually wrote may keep that original author; new bot work, bot amends that change the tree, and bot-authored replacements must use the agent identity.

This rule is requested and permanent. It wins over any instruction to "use the existing git config" when that config is a human.